of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}g(s)\\cos (4s)ds \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}f(\\frac{L}{π}s)\\cos (4s)ds \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{π} \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}x)\\cos (4 \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}x)d(\\frac{π}{L}x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos (\\frac{4π}{L}x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos (\\frac{4π}{L}x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, $4$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{4} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}g(s)\\sin (4s)ds \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}f(\\frac{L}{π}s)\\sin (4s)ds \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{π} \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}x)\\sin (4 \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}x)d(\\frac{π}{L}x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\sin (\\frac{4π}{L}x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\sin (\\frac{4π}{L}x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2$ and\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\cosh(x+1)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\cosh (x + 1)\\cos (\\frac{4π}{2}x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\cosh (x + 1)\\cos (2πx)dx, \\\\\nb_{4} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\cosh (x + 1)\\sin (\\frac{4π}{2}x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\cosh (x + 1)\\sin (2πx)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535637 false] [17592186221137 1145 "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k \\neq 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{L} = 2\\pi.$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\na_n\\cos\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)+\nb_n\\sin\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4$th cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, $4$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2$ and $f(x)=\\cosh(x+1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x, \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535637 true] [17592186221138 1100 "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3x^2-4x+1$ for $x\\in[-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_0.$" 13194139535637 true] [17592186221138 1100 "$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)$\nLet $F(x)=3x^2-4x+1$ for $[MATH: x\\in[-2,2).]$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_0$." 13194139535637 false] [17592186221138 1145 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2-4x+1\n$$\n\nfor $[MATH: x \\in [-2,2).]$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{0} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}(3x^{2} - 4x + 1)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[x^{3} - 2x^{2} + x]_{-2}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((2^{3} - 2 \\cdot 2^{2} + 2) - ((-2)^{3} - 2 \\cdot (-2)^{2} + (-2))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2 - (-18)) \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535637 false] [17592186221138 1145 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=3x^2-4x+1$ for $x \